WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning Hank, it's Wednesday.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.200
We seem to have found ourselves in something of a debt crisis, and by we I don't mean

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.260
like, the vlogbrothers, I mean, like, the planet.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.160
I've already talked about sovereign debt problems in Europe; today I want to talk about the

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.140
purported American debt crisis.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.920
But first let me define a few terms. There are basically

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.640
two kinds of debt. Unsecured debt is debt that you promise to pay back

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.000
but, uh, y'know, I have to take your word for it.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.800
And then there's collateralized debt like a mortgage,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.480
which is debt that you promise to pay back and if you don't pay it back I get your house.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.440
Generally, collateralized debt is safer than unsecured debt, which is why, like, mortgages

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.700
tend to be less expensive than credit cards, although as we have lately learned, collateralized debt

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.560
is not always a good investment.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.480
Another term, gross domestic product, is a measure of, like, the total annual economic output of a country.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.540
In 2010 the GDP of the United States was about 14.6 trillion dollars.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.080
That's a big number!

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.460
Unfortunately our total debt obligations are currently also 14.6 trillion dollars.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.740
This means that our debt to GDP ratio is about 100%.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.680
Which could be worse.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:58.760
[whispers]: It's not good.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.340
So Hank, traditionally when governments have a debt to GDP ratio over 90%

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.360
they tend to, I mean not always, but they tend to

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.180
default on that debt, like they tend to go bankrupt.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.340
Basically the government tells its lenders, "We promised we would pay you back but we were lying.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.020
Sorry life is hard and full of disappointment, here's a Tootsie Pop."

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.380
Just kidding, there are no Tootsie Pops.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.280
This is obviously a bad thing for the lenders but it's also

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.540
a bad thing for the government because

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.560
once you've proven that you're the kind of country that

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.020
occasionally goes bankrupt, people are reluctant to lend you money at low interest rates.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.720
But it still happens all the time. In the last few centuries

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.420
hundreds of governments have defaulted on their debt obligations and it doesn't matter if the currency is

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.940
backed by gold or silver or promises; it doesn't matter.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.540
All sovereign debt is essentially unsecured debt.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.480
If you loan the government money by purchasing a treasury bond

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.920
they don't have a house to pay you back with.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.500
And the price of gold and silver are so far removed

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.100
from their values as commodities they don't work as currency backers either.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.880
Many countries on the gold standard have defaulted

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.860
on their debts. There is only one thing backing

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.160
currency in this world, and that is trust.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.560
Okay, side note: in order to help investors to know whether something is credit-worthy

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.420
there are these independent, non-partisan, credit-rating agencies,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.680
like Standard and Poor's.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.120
They rate debt from triple A on down

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.540
and generally, just like individuals, the better your credit score the cheaper your debt.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.840
That's why Australia pays 4% for ten-year government bonds and Greece pays 15%.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.340
Sorry Greece.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.240
So the US is well above that 90% debt-to-GDP ratio that sets off alarm bells

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.760
and Congress, just barely, in the stupidest game of chicken ever,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.940
managed not to default on our debt.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.580
So the rating agency Standard and Poor's was like,

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:29.600
"You know, for the first time in US history, it's time to downgrade their debt from triple A to AA plus."

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:30.720
Here's what you'd expect to happen:

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.960
our debt being less secure becomes much more expensive.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.840
This can be disastrous because as your debt gets more expensive, you need more money to service it

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.720
so you have to raise taxes and cut spending, which can slow the economy and decrease government revenue.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.920
And then to make up the shortfall you have to acquire ever-more expensive debt until you go bankrupt.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.320
[chair rolling] But here's the thing, Hank: that's not what's happening in the United states.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.220
In the days since Standard and Poor's downgraded our credit rating, our debt hasn't gotten more expensive;

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.580
it's gotten cheaper.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.640
In fact Hank, the yield on some treasury bills is so low the United States is essentially getting paid to borrow money.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.700
Why? A few reasons:

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.240
First, the whole world economy is so voilatile right now that

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.420
even if US debt is less safe than it used to be, it still seems more safe than anything else.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.240
Two, our so-called debt crisis would almost completely be solved by just not extending the Bush tax cuts

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.380
which will happen if Congress does nothing,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.040
and, as you may have noticed, Congress is AWESOME at doing nothing.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.320
Three, the whole world is invested in the American economy.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.980
Hank, by some estimates, half of the American money in circulation, is in circulation outside the US.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.340
And lastly, at least for the time being, it's really important to the countries that make stuff,

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.320
like China, that countries that consume stuff, like the United States, keep consuming.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.620
So even though our debt's been downgraded it's still much cheaper than lots of countries with triple A ratings.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.380
Hank, the fact that the dollar remains the default currency of the world,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.500
and that people still trust the United states to pay its debts is worth trillions of dollars to us annually.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.180
Hank, that's probabaly not going to go away anytime soon,

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.680
but in the long run, every time we show an inability to get things done, politically or economically,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.880
we chip away at the world's faith in us.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.340
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

